DOS-PORT (patch 0096, Phase 9 wave 22.5 W22B). Decomposes the wave-22
W22A2-measured mode_draw_scene = 22.53 ms gameplay (84% of mode_tick
= THE primary lever per docs/PHASE11-WAVE-22-FINDINGS.md sec. 10 +
wave-23 candidate-E ranking) into 6 work-type sub-buckets covering
DrawScene() body at vendor/nxengine-evo/src/game.cpp:489-736.

Authored against docs/PHASE11-WAVE-22-5-CONTRACT.md (gitignored).
The wave-22.5 measurement gates wave-23 implementation: dominant
sub-bucket determines whether wave-23 attacks per-object batching
(if mds_object_loop dominates), tilemap caching (if mds_tilemap_bg
+ mds_tilemap_fg dominate), or other paths. Per
perf_predictions_unreliable.md + dosbox_scales_per_code_path.md --
measure first, optimize second. Pre-authoring batching against the
3-10 ms gain ceiling without knowing path-dominance was the trap
the wave-22 audit-first discipline was meant to prevent.

Implementation:

1. **SDL_HINT_DOSKUTSU_MODE_DRAW_SCENE_INSTR=1** env helper (strict-1,
   default OFF). NEW separate hint from W22A's MODE_TICK_INSTR per
   contract sec. 2 -- preserves the MODE_*_INSTR family-prefix
   consistency (FLIP_INSTR -> FINE_INSTR -> MODE_TICK_INSTR ->
   MODE_DRAW_SCENE_INSTR makes the layer hierarchy visible in env
   listings) and rejects strict-"1" violation of numeric escalation
   `=2` (one-way door breaking CLAUDE.md convention; same call as
   W22A sec. 2). Cached static-const-bool -- -O2 hoists per-tick guards.

2. **6 RAII timer structs** in src/Utils/diagnostic_timers.h,
   mirroring W22A's ModeSimTimer / ModeDrawSceneTimer etc. pattern:
   - MdsClearTimer            -- clearScreen(BLACK) at game.cpp:493
   - MdsTilemapBgTimer        -- map_draw_backdrop + map_draw(false) +
                                tintScreen at game.cpp:496-503
   - MdsObjectLoopTimer       -- the for-loop body at game.cpp:506-710
                                (per-object drawSprite +
                                 drawSpotLight switch over ~200
                                 sprite IDs)
   - MdsPlayerTimer           -- DrawPlayer() at game.cpp:713
   - MdsTilemapFgTimer        -- map_draw(TA_FOREGROUND) +
                                map_draw_oob() at game.cpp:716-721
   - MdsCaretsWaterFloatsTimer- Carets::DrawAll +
                                map_drawwaterlevel +
                                FloatText::DrawAll at
                                game.cpp:725-731

   Each gates on mode_draw_scene_instr_active() at destruction;
   same construction-time SDL_GetTicksNS read pattern as W22A.

3. **Storage** (file-scope in Renderer.cpp's NXE::Diag namespace,
   alongside W20B + W22A storage so audit-flush emits all 4
   instrumentation layers from a single TU):
   - 6 sample arrays + 6 idx counters
   - 1 residual sample array + 1 residual idx
   - 6 per-tick dt locals (reset at Game::tick entry; written by
     each timer's destructor; consumed by the new residual block)

   Reuses W22A's g_eng_tick_block_size = 100 (per contract sec. 3 -- no
   new block_size constant introduced).

4. **Residual derivation** at end-of-mode-draw-scene scope, gated
   separately from W20B + W22A residual blocks. Reuses W22A's
   g_mt_draw_scene_dt_this_tick (populated by ModeDrawSceneTimer
   in patch 0095) as the parent total -- NO new SDL_GetTicksNS read
   at end-of-tick.

       _mds_residual = g_mt_draw_scene_dt_this_tick
                     - mds_clear_dt - mds_tilemap_bg_dt
                     - mds_object_loop_dt - mds_player_dt
                     - mds_tilemap_fg_dt - mds_carets_water_floats_dt

   Signed math + clamp at 0. Self-validating: small mds_residual
   median (<1 ms) means brackets are exhaustive; large residual
   surfaces an unbracketed chunk inside DrawScene() body for
   wave-23 candidate-E refinement.

   CRITICAL PLACEMENT CONSTRAINT (per contract sec. 4 / sec. 8 #8 / sec. 9):
   the residual block lives in `game_tick_normal()` IMMEDIATELY
   AFTER the ModeDrawSceneTimer scope closes (BETWEEN that scope
   close and the ModeDrawHudTimer scope open), NOT at end-of-
   DrawScene() body. Reason: g_mt_draw_scene_dt_this_tick is only
   written by W22A's ModeDrawSceneTimer destructor at scope-exit,
   which fires INSIDE game_tick_normal() around the DrawScene()
   call -- NOT inside DrawScene() body itself. Reading the variable
   inside DrawScene() before the destructor fires would see stale
   data from prior tick. Same trap-and-fix structure as W22A's
   residual reading post-EngineTickTimer.

   COUPLING (mirrors W22A's coupling on FINE_INSTR): the W22.5
   residual block reads g_mt_draw_scene_dt_this_tick which is
   only written when MODE_TICK_INSTR=1 (W22A's gate). With
   MODE_DRAW_SCENE_INSTR=1 but MODE_TICK_INSTR=0, residual
   silently clamps to 0 every tick (sub-bucket samples remain
   valid). Operator's W22B iter (contract sec. 6) sets both hints,
   so this is not a real-world issue; documented in BOOT.md.

5. **Audit-flush emission** in _maybe_flush_phase_timing_audit():
   7 new

       [mode-draw-scene-stat n=N phase=<NAME> min=N.NN med=N.NN
        mean=N.NN p95=N.NN max=N.NN ms]

   lines per 100-tick block when MODE_DRAW_SCENE_INSTR=1. Distinct
   prefix `mode-draw-scene-stat` (vs W20A's `flip-stat`, W20B's
   `engine-tick-stat`, W22A's `mode-tick-stat`) so 4-layer grep
   tooling separates the layers without ambiguity. Empty-block
   placeholder lines (n=0, all numerics 0.00) emitted for the 6
   sub-buckets with zero firings -- critical for W22B1 title menu
   where ALL 6 sub-buckets fire n=0 (title's draw_title doesn't
   enter DrawScene). Residual line ALWAYS emits with sane numbers
   via the emit-gate construction (no placeholder branch needed).

   Order in the log per block: [flip-stat] (W20A) ->
   [engine-tick-stat] (W20B) -> [mode-tick-stat] (W22A) ->
   [mode-draw-scene-stat] (W22.5) -> counters. Outermost-to-
   innermost decomposition; greppable in priority order.

6. **Same _compute_flip_phase_stats helper** reused (W20A's
   sort/min/med/mean/p95/max/2-decimal infrastructure stays
   layer-agnostic; same _FlipPhaseStats struct). NO fourth-layer
   copy of the helper -- three or four copies would diverge over
   time per contract sec. 8 #2.

7. **Cross-namespace plumbing** mirrors W20B + W22A precedent:
   forward-declared free function NXE::Diag::mode_draw_scene_-
   instr_active() at namespace scope (alongside engine_tick_fine_-
   instr_active and mode_tick_instr_active in the file-top
   wrapper) with a 1-line definition at file scope after BOTH
   outer NXE::Graphics namespace braces close. Heeds the W20B
   build-fix amendment (per W22A precedent) -- placement at
   "after the anonymous namespace closes but BEFORE the outer
   NXE::Graphics closes" would re-open NXE inside NXE::Graphics,
   producing nested NXE::Graphics::NXE::Diag. File-scope-after-
   both-namespace-closes is the correct placement; same trap,
   same fix.

Bracket-placement decisions vs contract sec. 1 (transparent flag of
contract-text-vs-implementation deltas, per silent-narrow rule --
all 3 acked by flush-instr at Task #3-equivalent review):

a. game.cpp:567 `nOnscreenObjects = 0;` falls into mds_residual
   rather than mds_object_loop. Contract sec. 1 enumerated "lines
   506-710 (entire for-loop)" -- strictly the for-statement and
   its compound block. The 1-line int assignment immediately
   preceding is correctly outside scope. Sub-us work; residual
   absorbs without distorting the headline number. Same pattern
   as W22A's player-init flag at game_tick_normal().

b. game.cpp:552-553 DrawScene function-entry decls (`int scr_x,
   scr_y; extern int flipacceltime;`) fall into mds_residual.
   Pure stack allocation + extern decl; near-zero cost. Contract
   didn't enumerate these as a bracket.

c. MdsObjectLoopTimer scope brace style -- used a `{ ... }` block
   with internal comment `// end MdsObjectLoopTimer scope` to
   make the closing-brace identity visible, since the for-loop's
   `}` and the timer scope's `}` would otherwise look identical
   at a glance. Preference, not contract item.

Non-coverage (intentional, per contract sec. 1):
- Title menu's draw_title() NOT bracketed by W22.5 -- title's
  mode_draw_scene continues as a single number from W22A. Could
  be added later as a parallel mds_title_* scheme if W22B1
  reveals title-side work needs decomposition; not in scope here
  per silent-narrow rule.
- Within-object-loop sub-decomposition (drawSprite vs
  drawSpriteClipped vs drawSpotLight switch) NOT bracketed --
  wave-23 candidate-E's audit work itself will surface within-
  loop concentration if mds_object_loop dominates >50%.
- DrawBoundingBoxes (debug-only, off in production) NOT
  bracketed -- falls into mds_residual.

Hot-path cost when MODE_DRAW_SCENE_INSTR unset (mirrors W22A):
- 6 RAII destructors per DrawScene() invocation (each reads
  SDL_GetTicksNS at construction unconditionally; gating happens
  at destruction).
- Construction overhead: 6 SDL_GetTicksNS reads per DrawScene
  (paid only when game_tick_normal runs; 0x cost on title attract
  where DrawScene is never called). Negligible per the W22A
  precedent.
- ~4.3 KB unused static state.
- Residual block: gated on mode_draw_scene_instr_active(); 0 cost
  when env unset.

Documentation: docs/BOOT.md gets a comprehensive
MODE_DRAW_SCENE_INSTR entry covering bracket scheme, residual
semantics + critical placement constraint, empty-block
placeholder behavior, MODE_TICK_INSTR pairing dependency,
non-coverage scope, and strict-match convention. BOOT.md is
gitignored per pre-existing convention (.gitignore:95) --
internal-only docs that live on-disk for reference, not in the
public repo. Same situation as W20B's FINE_INSTR + W22A's
MODE_TICK_INSTR doc trail.

